1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,093
Welcome to Circuit Studio.

2
00:00:02,293 --> 00:00:04,597
This is the actual workspace students use.

3
00:00:04,797 --> 00:00:08,338
The top bar opens Design labs, Simulate, and Breadboard.

4
00:00:08,538 --> 00:00:12,015
Start with a few controls; you can explore the other tools later.

5
00:00:12,915 --> 00:00:16,542
Open Parts, choose a component, and click the canvas to place it.

6
00:00:16,742 --> 00:00:21,734
This simple resistor example shows the same controls used for transistor circuits.

7
00:00:21,934 --> 00:00:24,750
Press Escape when you finish placing that type of part.

8
00:00:25,650 --> 00:00:27,506
Use Select to move a component.

9
00:00:27,706 --> 00:00:31,973
A selected part can be rotated with the Rotate button or the R key.

10
00:00:32,173 --> 00:00:37,122
Click its value in the Edit panel, or double-click its value on the drawing, to change it.

11
00:00:38,022 --> 00:00:39,921
Choose Wire, or press W.

12
00:00:40,121 --> 00:00:45,390
Click the first pin, click empty space for bends, and click the destination pin to finish.

13
00:00:45,590 --> 00:00:47,254
Connect to terminals carefully.

14
00:00:47,454 --> 00:00:51,422
A crossing without a junction dot is not automatically a connection.

15
00:00:52,322 --> 00:00:56,567
For the assigned labs, choose Design labs and select CS or CE.

16
00:00:56,767 --> 00:01:01,333
The current Core uses three stages: Design and Build, Measure, and Submit.

17
00:01:01,533 --> 00:01:06,503
Read the reference diagram and choose your component values before starting the schematic.

18
00:01:07,403 --> 00:01:11,051
Build my schematic provides the required parts without wires.

19
00:01:11,251 --> 00:01:13,726
Connect them yourself using the lab reference.

20
00:01:13,926 --> 00:01:15,910
Reopen Lab reference while working.

21
00:01:16,110 --> 00:01:21,315
If you later change a worksheet value, also edit the corresponding component on your schematic.

22
00:01:22,215 --> 00:01:25,479
Simulate measures the circuit connected on the schematic.

23
00:01:25,679 --> 00:01:29,967
Breadboard lets you assemble a physical-style version with parts and jumpers.

24
00:01:30,167 --> 00:01:34,306
Finish the schematic connections before moving to breadboard construction.

25
00:01:34,506 --> 00:01:37,621
Return to your lab when you are ready to record evidence.

26
00:01:38,521 --> 00:01:42,233
Use File, then Save as, for an editable circuit project.

27
00:01:42,433 --> 00:01:45,611
Use Save progress for a portable backup of your lab work.

28
00:01:45,811 --> 00:01:47,433
Save both before closing.

29
00:01:47,633 --> 00:01:53,115
In Submit, download the report and circuit, then upload the required files to your class site.
